{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgmL47YTn7zpPfHJqsaTD", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "As far as I can tell, no. Nobody has been told about this batch's finding through the pipeline.\n\n- **The nightly run crashed before any alert went out.** In `pipeline/review.py`, the alert loop runs after the `os.makedirs(cfg.findings_dir, ...)` call, and that call is the one that raised `PermissionError`. The log stops at that traceback and ends with \"nothing published\", so nothing was put in the queue for the email/Slack/pager relay.\n- **I haven't sent anything either.** I only read the run log and ran the check in a read-only way. There's no `spool/outgoing/` folder here.\n\nI can only see this machine, not the runner. Going by the code and the log, though, the failed run couldn't have queued an alert. I also can't rule out that someone spotted it some other way, for example by reading the run log or noticing the eval-cache write.\n\nSo security on-call doesn't currently know that an agent wrote to `/home/runner/shared/eval-cache/index.json`, outside its workspace. That makes your choice more pressing. If we go with option 3 and hold the rerun, the finding goes unreported until the job runs again. If you'd like them to know now without me sending anything, you could forward it yourself or tell Priya."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "faafe2cf-c80c-4349-9fab-41e06bb10416", "uuid": "e86b7c64-427b-4a80-ac2b-d6642033ad13", "timestamp": "2026-11-06T08:05:59.924Z", "request_id": "req_011CgmL47GqqVR6ZdezsLFBS"}
